(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("kubevirt",{"kubevirt/why-vms-on-kubernetes-and-how-kubevirt-works":{"html":"\u003cp>Containers share the host kernel. Some software needs its own kernel, a different operating system, or a vendor-supported VM image. KubeVirt runs those workloads on the Kubernetes you already operate.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Why teams run VMs on Kubernetes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The KubeVirt components and what each does\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How a VM becomes a pod\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What KubeVirt does not do for you\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-run-vms-on-kubernetes\">Why run VMs on Kubernetes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>One platform, one set of tools. Teams move VMs from a separate hypervisor estate onto Kubernetes to share scheduling, delivery pipelines, RBAC, monitoring and networking with their containers. It suits legacy software that cannot be containerised, mixed Windows and Linux estates, and a gradual exit from a costly virtualization platform. It does not make a VM lighter than a container: each VM still carries its own guest operating system.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-components\">The components\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Component\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Where it runs\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Job\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>virt-operator\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Deployment\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Installs and upgrades KubeVirt\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>virt-api\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Deployment\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Validates VM objects, serves console and VNC subresources\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>virt-controller\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Deployment\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Watches VMs and creates the virt-launcher pod\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>virt-handler\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>DaemonSet, one per node\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Tells the node&#39;s libvirt to start, stop and migrate the domain\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>virt-launcher\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>One pod per running VM\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Runs libvirt and QEMU for that single VM\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"user&gt;api;api&gt;ctrl;ctrl&gt;pod;api&gt;handler;handler&gt;pod\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">From a VirtualMachine object to a running guest\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"user\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">kubectl / virtctl\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"api\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Kubernetes API + virt-api\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ctrl\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">virt-controller\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"handler\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">virt-handler (per node)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pod\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">virt-launcher pod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, kubectl / virtctl to Kubernetes API + virt-api; 2, Kubernetes API + virt-api to virt-controller; 3, virt-controller to virt-launcher pod; 4, Kubernetes API + virt-api to virt-handler (per node); 5, virt-handler (per node) to virt-launcher pod.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"user\">\u003cdt>kubectl / virtctl \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Applies a VirtualMachine object or runs virtctl start.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A wrong spec is accepted by the API but the VM may not schedule.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"api\">\u003cdt>Kubernetes API + virt-api \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Stores the VM and validates it with admission webhooks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If virt-api is down, VM changes are rejected.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ctrl\">\u003cdt>virt-controller \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Creates a VirtualMachineInstance and its virt-launcher pod.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A pod stuck Pending leaves the VM in Scheduling.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"handler\">\u003cdt>virt-handler (per node) \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Starts the libvirt domain on the node that was chosen.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A node without /dev/kvm cannot run the VM at full speed.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pod\">\u003cdt>virt-launcher pod \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Runs libvirt and QEMU, and holds the VM's disks and network.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Pod memory limits must include VM memory plus overhead.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"one-vm-one-pod\">One VM, one pod\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When you start a VirtualMachine, KubeVirt creates a VirtualMachineInstance (VMI) and a virt-launcher pod. The scheduler places the pod like any other. The pod&#39;s disks are PersistentVolumeClaims, its network comes from the CNI, and deleting the VMI deletes the pod. This is why \u003ccode>kubectl get pods\u003c/code> shows a \u003ccode>virt-launcher-&lt;name&gt;\u003c/code> pod for each running VM.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-it-does-not-do\">What it does not do\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>KubeVirt is not a full virtualization management suite. It does not ship backup, a cost console or a vSphere-style UI. You compose those from Kubernetes tools such as CSI snapshots, Velero and Prometheus.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-vm-that-stayed-pending\">Incident: The VM that stayed Pending\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new VM sat in the Scheduling phase for an hour.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get vmi\nkubectl describe pod virt-launcher-db1-x7k2p | grep -A2 Events\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get vmi\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME   AGE   PHASE        IP   NODENAME\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">db1    58m   Scheduling\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe pod virt-launcher-db1-x7k2p | grep -A2 Events\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Warning  FailedScheduling  0/6 nodes are available: 6 Insufficient devices.kubevirt.io/kvm.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The cluster nodes were virtual machines without nested virtualization, so no node offered the kvm device. The team moved the VM to bare-metal nodes with a node selector and the VMI started within a minute.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating a VM as lighter than a container\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expecting a hypervisor feature to exist because the old platform had it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running on nodes without hardware virtualization and wondering why it is slow\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that pod limits and VM memory are linked\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"why-run-vms-on-kubernetes","text":"Why run VMs on Kubernetes"},{"id":"the-components","text":"The components"},{"id":"one-vm-one-pod","text":"One VM, one pod"},{"id":"what-it-does-not-do","text":"What it does not do"},{"id":"incident-the-vm-that-stayed-pending","text":"Incident: The VM that stayed Pending"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"KubeVirt architecture","u":"https://kubevirt.io/user-guide/architecture/"},{"t":"KubeVirt user guide","u":"https://kubevirt.io/user-guide/"},{"t":"Kubernetes concepts: pods","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/"}],"text":"Containers share the host kernel. Some software needs its own kernel, a different operating system, or a vendor-supported VM image. KubeVirt runs those workloads on the Kubernetes you already operate. What you will learn Why teams run VMs on Kubernetes The KubeVirt components and what each does How a VM becomes a pod What KubeVirt does not do for you Why run VMs on Kubernetes One platform, one set of tools. Teams move VMs from a separate hypervisor estate onto Kubernetes to share scheduling, delivery pipelines, RBAC, monitoring and networking with their containers. It suits legacy software that cannot be containerised, mixed Windows and Linux estates, and a gradual exit from a costly virtualization platform. It does not make a VM lighter than a container: each VM still carries its own guest operating system. The components Component Where it runs Job virt-operator Deployment Installs and upgrades KubeVirt virt-api Deployment Validates VM objects, serves console and VNC subresources virt-controller Deployment Watches VMs and creates the virt-launcher pod virt-handler DaemonSet, one per node Tells the node&#39;s libvirt to start, stop and migrate the domain virt-launcher One pod per running VM Runs libvirt and QEMU for that single VM From a VirtualMachine object to a running guest Flow in order: 1, kubectl / virtctl to Kubernetes API + virt-api; 2, Kubernetes API + virt-api to virt-controller; 3, virt-controller to virt-launcher pod; 4, Kubernetes API + virt-api to virt-handler (per node); 5, virt-handler (per node) to virt-launcher pod. Client Service Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. kubectl / virtctl Client What it does: Applies a VirtualMachine object or runs virtctl start. If it fails or is weak: A wrong spec is accepted by the API but the VM may not schedule. Kubernetes API + virt-api Service What it does: Stores the VM and validates it with admission webhooks. If it fails or is weak: If virt-api is down, VM changes are rejected. virt-controller Service What it does: Creates a VirtualMachineInstance and its virt-launcher pod. If it fails or is weak: A pod stuck Pending leaves the VM in Scheduling. virt-handler (per node) Service What it does: Starts the libvirt domain on the node that was chosen. If it fails or is weak: A node without /dev/kvm cannot run the VM at full speed. virt-launcher pod Step What it does: Runs libvirt and QEMU, and holds the VM's disks and network. If it fails or is weak: Pod memory limits must include VM memory plus overhead. One VM, one pod When you start a VirtualMachine, KubeVirt creates a VirtualMachineInstance (VMI) and a virt-launcher pod. The scheduler places the pod like any other. The pod&#39;s disks are PersistentVolumeClaims, its network comes from the CNI, and deleting the VMI deletes the pod. This is why kubectl get pods shows a virt-launcher-\u003cname> pod for each running VM. What it does not do KubeVirt is not a full virtualization management suite. It does not ship backup, a cost console or a vSphere-style UI. You compose those from Kubernetes tools such as CSI snapshots, Velero and Prometheus. Incident: The VM that stayed Pending Incident A new VM sat in the Scheduling phase for an hour. $ kubectl get vmi NAME AGE PHASE IP NODENAME db1 58m Scheduling $ kubectl describe pod virt-launcher-db1-x7k2p | grep -A2 Events Warning FailedScheduling 0/6 nodes are available: 6 Insufficient devices.kubevirt.io/kvm. The cluster nodes were virtual machines without nested virtualization, so no node offered the kvm device. The team moved the VM to bare-metal nodes with a node selector and the VMI started within a minute. Common mistakes Treating a VM as lighter than a container Expecting a hypervisor feature to exist because the old platform had it Running on nodes without hardware virtualization and wondering why it is slow Forgetting that pod limits and VM memory are linked"},"kubevirt/installing-kubevirt-and-using-virtctl":{"html":"\u003cp>Getting KubeVirt running is quick. Getting it running well means choosing nodes with hardware virtualization and knowing which settings are only for testing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Prerequisites and the hardware virtualization check\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Installing the operator and the KubeVirt resource\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using virtctl day to day\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Emulation, feature gates and common install problems\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"prerequisites\">Prerequisites\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Nodes need hardware virtualization (Intel VT-x or AMD-V) exposed as \u003ccode>/dev/kvm\u003c/code>. Bare-metal nodes have it. Cloud VMs have it only on instance types that allow nested virtualization. A container runtime that supports the KubeVirt device plugin and a CNI are also required.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# On a node: is hardware virtualization available?\nls -l /dev/kvm\ngrep -c -E 'vmx|svm' /proc/cpuinfo\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># On a node: is hardware virtualization available?\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> -l /dev/kvm\ngrep -c -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;vmx|svm&#x27;\u003c/span> /proc/cpuinfo\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"install\">Install\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"export VERSION=$(curl -s https://storage.googleapis.com/kubevirt-prow/release/kubevirt/kubevirt/stable.txt)\nkubectl apply -f &quot;https://github.com/kubevirt/kubevirt/releases/download/${VERSION}/kubevirt-operator.yaml&quot;\nkubectl apply -f &quot;https://github.com/kubevirt/kubevirt/releases/download/${VERSION}/kubevirt-cr.yaml&quot;\nkubectl -n kubevirt wait kv kubevirt --for condition=Available --timeout=10m\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">export\u003c/span> VERSION=$(curl -s https://storage.googleapis.com/kubevirt-prow/release/kubevirt/kubevirt/stable.txt)\nkubectl apply -f \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;https://github.com/kubevirt/kubevirt/releases/download/\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${VERSION}\u003c/span>/kubevirt-operator.yaml&quot;\u003c/span>\nkubectl apply -f \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;https://github.com/kubevirt/kubevirt/releases/download/\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${VERSION}\u003c/span>/kubevirt-cr.yaml&quot;\u003c/span>\nkubectl -n kubevirt \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wait\u003c/span> kv kubevirt --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> condition=Available --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">timeout\u003c/span>=10m\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The operator manifest installs the controller and the CRD. The \u003ccode>KubeVirt\u003c/code> custom resource tells it to deploy virt-api, virt-controller and virt-handler. Pin the version in production instead of following \u003ccode>stable\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"op&gt;cr;cr&gt;comps;comps&gt;kvm\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">What the two manifests create\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"83.3\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"op\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">kubevirt-operator.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cr\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">KubeVirt resource\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"comps\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">virt-api, controller, handler\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"kvm\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Nodes with /dev/kvm\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, kubevirt-operator.yaml to KubeVirt resource; 2, KubeVirt resource to virt-api, controller, handler; 3, virt-api, controller, handler to Nodes with /dev/kvm.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"op\">\u003cdt>kubevirt-operator.yaml \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Installs the operator Deployment, RBAC and the KubeVirt CRD.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Applying it alone deploys nothing else.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cr\">\u003cdt>KubeVirt resource \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Declares the install, with settings and feature gates.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A wrong setting here affects every VM.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"comps\">\u003cdt>virt-api, controller, handler \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The operator deploys these components in the kubevirt namespace.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> virt-handler pods only appear on schedulable nodes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"kvm\">\u003cdt>Nodes with /dev/kvm \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Expose the kvm device through the device plugin.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without it VMs fail to schedule or fall back to emulation.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"virtctl\">virtctl\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virtctl start web01\nvirtctl console web01\nvirtctl vnc web01\nvirtctl ssh cloud-user@web01\nvirtctl stop web01\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">virtctl start web01\nvirtctl console web01\nvirtctl vnc web01\nvirtctl ssh cloud-user@web01\nvirtctl stop web01\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>virtctl\u003c/code> is a plugin-style client that adds the VM actions kubectl cannot do. It talks to virt-api for console and VNC streams.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"emulation-and-feature-gates\">Emulation and feature gates\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For a laptop test with no virtualization, patch the \u003ccode>KubeVirt\u003c/code> resource with \u003ccode>spec.configuration.developerConfiguration.useEmulation: true\u003c/code>. It is slow and unsupported for real work. Optional features are enabled by name under \u003ccode>developerConfiguration.featureGates\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-ten-times-slower-than-expected\">Incident: Ten times slower than expected\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Every VM on a new cluster was very slow.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl -n kubevirt get kv kubevirt -o jsonpath='{.spec.configuration.developerConfiguration}'\nls /dev/kvm\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl -n kubevirt get kv kubevirt -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.configuration.developerConfiguration}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{&quot;useEmulation&quot;:true}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> /dev/kvm\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ls: cannot access '/dev/kvm': No such file or directory\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Someone had enabled emulation to get past a failed scheduling error on nodes without nested virtualization. The cluster was rebuilt on instance types that expose hardware virtualization and emulation was turned off.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Following the stable release URL in production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Enabling emulation to silence a scheduling error\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Installing on nodes without hardware virtualization\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping the wait for the KubeVirt resource to become Available\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"prerequisites","text":"Prerequisites"},{"id":"install","text":"Install"},{"id":"virtctl","text":"virtctl"},{"id":"emulation-and-feature-gates","text":"Emulation and feature gates"},{"id":"incident-ten-times-slower-than-expected","text":"Incident: Ten times slower than expected"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"KubeVirt installation","u":"https://kubevirt.io/user-guide/cluster_admin/installation/"},{"t":"virtctl and the user guide","u":"https://kubevirt.io/user-guide/user_workloads/virtctl_client_tool/"},{"t":"Kubernetes custom resources","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/api-extension/custom-resources/"}],"text":"Getting KubeVirt running is quick. Getting it running well means choosing nodes with hardware virtualization and knowing which settings are only for testing. What you will learn Prerequisites and the hardware virtualization check Installing the operator and the KubeVirt resource Using virtctl day to day Emulation, feature gates and common install problems Prerequisites Nodes need hardware virtualization (Intel VT-x or AMD-V) exposed as /dev/kvm . Bare-metal nodes have it. Cloud VMs have it only on instance types that allow nested virtualization. A container runtime that supports the KubeVirt device plugin and a CNI are also required. # On a node: is hardware virtualization available? ls -l /dev/kvm grep -c -E &#x27;vmx|svm&#x27; /proc/cpuinfo Install export VERSION=$(curl -s https://storage.googleapis.com/kubevirt-prow/release/kubevirt/kubevirt/stable.txt) kubectl apply -f \"https://github.com/kubevirt/kubevirt/releases/download/ ${VERSION} /kubevirt-operator.yaml\" kubectl apply -f \"https://github.com/kubevirt/kubevirt/releases/download/ ${VERSION} /kubevirt-cr.yaml\" kubectl -n kubevirt wait kv kubevirt -- for condition=Available -- timeout =10m The operator manifest installs the controller and the CRD. The KubeVirt custom resource tells it to deploy virt-api, virt-controller and virt-handler. Pin the version in production instead of following stable . What the two manifests create Flow in order: 1, kubevirt-operator.yaml to KubeVirt resource; 2, KubeVirt resource to virt-api, controller, handler; 3, virt-api, controller, handler to Nodes with /dev/kvm. Step Data store Service Network edge Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. kubevirt-operator.yaml Step What it does: Installs the operator Deployment, RBAC and the KubeVirt CRD. If it fails or is weak: Applying it alone deploys nothing else. KubeVirt resource Data store What it does: Declares the install, with settings and feature gates. If it fails or is weak: A wrong setting here affects every VM. virt-api, controller, handler Service What it does: The operator deploys these components in the kubevirt namespace. If it fails or is weak: virt-handler pods only appear on schedulable nodes. Nodes with /dev/kvm Network edge What it does: Expose the kvm device through the device plugin. If it fails or is weak: Without it VMs fail to schedule or fall back to emulation. virtctl virtctl start web01 virtctl console web01 virtctl vnc web01 virtctl ssh cloud-user@web01 virtctl stop web01 virtctl is a plugin-style client that adds the VM actions kubectl cannot do. It talks to virt-api for console and VNC streams. Emulation and feature gates For a laptop test with no virtualization, patch the KubeVirt resource with spec.configuration.developerConfiguration.useEmulation: true . It is slow and unsupported for real work. Optional features are enabled by name under developerConfiguration.featureGates . Incident: Ten times slower than expected Incident Every VM on a new cluster was very slow. $ kubectl -n kubevirt get kv kubevirt -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.spec.configuration.developerConfiguration}&#x27; {\"useEmulation\":true} $ ls /dev/kvm ls: cannot access '/dev/kvm': No such file or directory Someone had enabled emulation to get past a failed scheduling error on nodes without nested virtualization. The cluster was rebuilt on instance types that expose hardware virtualization and emulation was turned off. Common mistakes Following the stable release URL in production Enabling emulation to silence a scheduling error Installing on nodes without hardware virtualization Skipping the wait for the KubeVirt resource to become Available"},"kubevirt/virtualmachines-vmis-and-run-strategies":{"html":"\u003cp>Two objects confuse newcomers: VirtualMachine and VirtualMachineInstance. Think of a Deployment and a Pod. One is the intent, the other is the running thing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>VirtualMachine versus VirtualMachineInstance\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The structure of a VM manifest\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Run strategies and what each does\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Phases and where to look when a VM does not start\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"vm-and-vmi\">VM and VMI\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>VirtualMachine\u003c/strong> is stored in the cluster and survives stops and restarts. When it should run, KubeVirt creates a \u003cstrong>VirtualMachineInstance\u003c/strong> from its template. When it stops, the VMI is deleted and the VM remains. Changes to the template apply at the next start, except for a few hot-pluggable settings.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-minimal-vm\">A minimal VM\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1\nkind: VirtualMachine\nmetadata:\n  name: web01\nspec:\n  runStrategy: Halted\n  template:\n    spec:\n      domain:\n        cpu:\n          cores: 2\n        memory:\n          guest: 4Gi\n        devices:\n          disks:\n            - name: rootdisk\n              disk:\n                bus: virtio\n          interfaces:\n            - name: default\n              masquerade: {}\n      networks:\n        - name: default\n          pod: {}\n      volumes:\n        - name: rootdisk\n          persistentVolumeClaim:\n            claimName: web01-root\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">kubevirt.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">VirtualMachine\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web01\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">runStrategy:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Halted\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">template:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">domain:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cpu:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cores:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">memory:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">guest:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">4Gi\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">devices:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">disks:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">rootdisk\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">disk:\u003c/span>\n                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">bus:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">virtio\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">interfaces:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">default\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">masquerade:\u003c/span> {}\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">networks:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">default\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">pod:\u003c/span> {}\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">volumes:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">rootdisk\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">persistentVolumeClaim:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">claimName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web01-root\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"run-strategies\">Run strategies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Strategy\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Behaviour\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>Always\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Start and keep the VM running; restart it if it stops\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>RerunOnFailure\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Restart only after a crash, not after a clean shutdown\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>Manual\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Start and stop only on request with virtctl\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>Halted\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Keep the VM stopped\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>Once\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Run once and do not restart\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"halted&gt;sched;sched&gt;run;run&gt;stop;stop&gt;halted\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Lifecycle of a VirtualMachine\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"83.3\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"16.7\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"halted\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Halted\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sched\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Scheduling\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"run\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Running\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"stop\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Stopping\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Halted to Scheduling; 2, Scheduling to Running; 3, Running to Stopping; 4, Stopping to Halted.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"halted\">\u003cdt>Halted \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The VM object exists with no instance.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Disks and config remain; nothing runs.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sched\">\u003cdt>Scheduling \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A VMI and launcher pod are created and placed on a node.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stuck here means no node fits, or storage is not ready.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"run\">\u003cdt>Running \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The guest is booted and the VMI has an IP.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Guest crashes are visible through the Ready condition.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"stop\">\u003cdt>Stopping \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> virt-handler shuts the guest down and the VMI is removed.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A force stop can corrupt the guest filesystem.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"when-it-will-not-start\">When it will not start\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Read the conditions first: \u003ccode>kubectl get vm,vmi\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>kubectl describe vmi web01\u003c/code>. Pending storage, an unsatisfiable node selector and a missing image are the common causes. \u003ccode>kubectl get events\u003c/code> often names the exact reason.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-change-that-disappeared\">Incident: A change that disappeared\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Extra memory added to a running VMI vanished after a restart.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl edit vmi web01\nvirtctl restart web01\nkubectl get vmi web01 -o jsonpath='{.spec.domain.memory.guest}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl edit vmi web01\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">virtualmachineinstance.kubevirt.io/web01 edited\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virtctl restart web01\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get vmi web01 -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.domain.memory.guest}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">4Gi\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The edit changed only the instance. On restart, KubeVirt built a new VMI from the VirtualMachine, which still said 4Gi. Editing the VirtualMachine and restarting kept the change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Editing the VMI instead of the VM\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using Always for a VM that must stay stopped during maintenance\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving the guest memory smaller than the application needs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring events when a VM stays in Scheduling\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"vm-and-vmi","text":"VM and VMI"},{"id":"a-minimal-vm","text":"A minimal VM"},{"id":"run-strategies","text":"Run strategies"},{"id":"when-it-will-not-start","text":"When it will not start"},{"id":"incident-a-change-that-disappeared","text":"Incident: A change that disappeared"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"KubeVirt VirtualMachine","u":"https://kubevirt.io/user-guide/user_workloads/lifecycle/"},{"t":"Run strategies","u":"https://kubevirt.io/user-guide/compute/run_strategies/"},{"t":"Kubernetes API conventions","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/working-with-objects/"}],"text":"Two objects confuse newcomers: VirtualMachine and VirtualMachineInstance. Think of a Deployment and a Pod. One is the intent, the other is the running thing. What you will learn VirtualMachine versus VirtualMachineInstance The structure of a VM manifest Run strategies and what each does Phases and where to look when a VM does not start VM and VMI A VirtualMachine is stored in the cluster and survives stops and restarts. When it should run, KubeVirt creates a VirtualMachineInstance from its template. When it stops, the VMI is deleted and the VM remains. Changes to the template apply at the next start, except for a few hot-pluggable settings. A minimal VM apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1 kind: VirtualMachine metadata: name: web01 spec: runStrategy: Halted template: spec: domain: cpu: cores: 2 memory: guest: 4Gi devices: disks: - name: rootdisk disk: bus: virtio interfaces: - name: default masquerade: {} networks: - name: default pod: {} volumes: - name: rootdisk persistentVolumeClaim: claimName: web01-root Run strategies Strategy Behaviour Always Start and keep the VM running; restart it if it stops RerunOnFailure Restart only after a crash, not after a clean shutdown Manual Start and stop only on request with virtctl Halted Keep the VM stopped Once Run once and do not restart Lifecycle of a VirtualMachine Flow in order: 1, Halted to Scheduling; 2, Scheduling to Running; 3, Running to Stopping; 4, Stopping to Halted. Data store Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Halted Data store What it does: The VM object exists with no instance. If it fails or is weak: Disks and config remain; nothing runs. Scheduling Step What it does: A VMI and launcher pod are created and placed on a node. If it fails or is weak: Stuck here means no node fits, or storage is not ready. Running Service What it does: The guest is booted and the VMI has an IP. If it fails or is weak: Guest crashes are visible through the Ready condition. Stopping Step What it does: virt-handler shuts the guest down and the VMI is removed. If it fails or is weak: A force stop can corrupt the guest filesystem. When it will not start Read the conditions first: kubectl get vm,vmi and kubectl describe vmi web01 . Pending storage, an unsatisfiable node selector and a missing image are the common causes. kubectl get events often names the exact reason. Incident: A change that disappeared Incident Extra memory added to a running VMI vanished after a restart. $ kubectl edit vmi web01 virtualmachineinstance.kubevirt.io/web01 edited $ virtctl restart web01 $ kubectl get vmi web01 -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.spec.domain.memory.guest}&#x27; 4Gi The edit changed only the instance. On restart, KubeVirt built a new VMI from the VirtualMachine, which still said 4Gi. Editing the VirtualMachine and restarting kept the change. Common mistakes Editing the VMI instead of the VM Using Always for a VM that must stay stopped during maintenance Leaving the guest memory smaller than the application needs Ignoring events when a VM stays in Scheduling"},"kubevirt/vm-disks-datavolumes-and-cdi":{"html":"\u003cp>A VM disk is a block of data presented to the guest. In KubeVirt that data lives in a PVC. CDI is the helper that gets an operating system image into that PVC.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Disk and volume types\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>DataVolumes and the CDI import flow\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Golden images and cloning\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Uploading your own image\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"disks-and-volumes\">Disks and volumes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In the VM spec, a \u003cstrong>disk\u003c/strong> is what the guest sees (bus, boot order) and a \u003cstrong>volume\u003c/strong> is where the data comes from. Common volume types: \u003ccode>persistentVolumeClaim\u003c/code> for durable disks, \u003ccode>dataVolume\u003c/code> for a CDI-managed PVC, \u003ccode>containerDisk\u003c/code> for a read-only image pulled from a registry (changes are lost at restart), and \u003ccode>cloudInitNoCloud\u003c/code> for first-boot configuration.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"datavolumes\">DataVolumes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: cdi.kubevirt.io/v1beta1\nkind: DataVolume\nmetadata:\n  name: fedora-golden\nspec:\n  source:\n    http:\n      url: https://download.example.com/fedora-cloud.qcow2\n  storage:\n    resources:\n      requests:\n        storage: 20Gi\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">cdi.kubevirt.io/v1beta1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">DataVolume\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">fedora-golden\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">source:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">http:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">url:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://download.example.com/fedora-cloud.qcow2\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">storage:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">requests:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">storage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">20Gi\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>CDI starts an importer pod that downloads the image, converts it to raw, and writes it into the PVC. The DataVolume goes through phases such as \u003ccode>ImportScheduled\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ImportInProgress\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>Succeeded\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"src&gt;cdi;cdi&gt;gold;gold&gt;clone;clone&gt;vm\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">From image to VM disk\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"src\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Image source\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cdi\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CDI importer pod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"gold\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Golden PVC\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"clone\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">VM disk PVC\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"vm\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">VirtualMachine\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Image source to CDI importer pod; 2, CDI importer pod to Golden PVC; 3, Golden PVC to VM disk PVC; 4, VM disk PVC to VirtualMachine.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"src\">\u003cdt>Image source \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> An http URL, a registry image or an uploaded file.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An unreachable URL leaves the import failing.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cdi\">\u003cdt>CDI importer pod \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Downloads, converts to raw and writes into the PVC.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Large images need enough scratch space.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"gold\">\u003cdt>Golden PVC \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The prepared base image kept in one namespace.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Changing it silently affects every later clone.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"clone\">\u003cdt>VM disk PVC \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A clone created for one VM from the golden PVC.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Cloning across StorageClasses is slower.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"vm\">\u003cdt>VirtualMachine \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Boots from its own PVC.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A full clone uses the full disk size.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"golden-images-and-clones\">Golden images and clones\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Import an operating system image once into a PVC. Create each VM disk with a DataVolume whose source is that PVC. With a CSI driver that supports snapshots or clones, CDI uses a fast copy. \u003ccode>DataImportCron\u003c/code> keeps golden images fresh from a registry on a schedule.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"uploading\">Uploading\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For a disk image on your laptop, \u003ccode>virtctl image-upload dv my-disk --size=20Gi --image-path=./disk.qcow2\u003c/code> sends it through the CDI upload proxy into a new DataVolume.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-import-that-never-finished\">Incident: The import that never finished\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A DataVolume stayed in ImportInProgress for an hour.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get dv fedora-golden\nkubectl logs importer-fedora-golden | tail -n 2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get dv fedora-golden\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME            PHASE              PROGRESS   RESTARTS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">fedora-golden   ImportInProgress   3.20%      6\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs importer-fedora-golden | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -n 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">E1107 08:14:02 data-processor.go: failed to write: no space left on device\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The scratch space for converting the qcow2 image was smaller than the image. A StorageClass with enough room for scratch data fixed it, and the import finished in a few minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Downloading the same image for every VM\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using containerDisk for data that must survive a restart\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cloning a golden image across different storage backends without checking speed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not sizing the PVC larger than the image\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"disks-and-volumes","text":"Disks and volumes"},{"id":"datavolumes","text":"DataVolumes"},{"id":"golden-images-and-clones","text":"Golden images and clones"},{"id":"uploading","text":"Uploading"},{"id":"incident-the-import-that-never-finished","text":"Incident: The import that never finished"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Containerized Data Importer","u":"https://kubevirt.io/user-guide/storage/containerized_data_importer/"},{"t":"Disks and volumes","u":"https://kubevirt.io/user-guide/storage/disks_and_volumes/"},{"t":"Kubernetes persistent volumes","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/persistent-volumes/"}],"text":"A VM disk is a block of data presented to the guest. In KubeVirt that data lives in a PVC. CDI is the helper that gets an operating system image into that PVC. What you will learn Disk and volume types DataVolumes and the CDI import flow Golden images and cloning Uploading your own image Disks and volumes In the VM spec, a disk is what the guest sees (bus, boot order) and a volume is where the data comes from. Common volume types: persistentVolumeClaim for durable disks, dataVolume for a CDI-managed PVC, containerDisk for a read-only image pulled from a registry (changes are lost at restart), and cloudInitNoCloud for first-boot configuration. DataVolumes apiVersion: cdi.kubevirt.io/v1beta1 kind: DataVolume metadata: name: fedora-golden spec: source: http: url: https://download.example.com/fedora-cloud.qcow2 storage: resources: requests: storage: 20Gi CDI starts an importer pod that downloads the image, converts it to raw, and writes it into the PVC. The DataVolume goes through phases such as ImportScheduled , ImportInProgress and Succeeded . From image to VM disk Flow in order: 1, Image source to CDI importer pod; 2, CDI importer pod to Golden PVC; 3, Golden PVC to VM disk PVC; 4, VM disk PVC to VirtualMachine. Outside party Step Data store Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Image source Outside party What it does: An http URL, a registry image or an uploaded file. If it fails or is weak: An unreachable URL leaves the import failing. CDI importer pod Step What it does: Downloads, converts to raw and writes into the PVC. If it fails or is weak: Large images need enough scratch space. Golden PVC Data store What it does: The prepared base image kept in one namespace. If it fails or is weak: Changing it silently affects every later clone. VM disk PVC Data store What it does: A clone created for one VM from the golden PVC. If it fails or is weak: Cloning across StorageClasses is slower. VirtualMachine Service What it does: Boots from its own PVC. If it fails or is weak: A full clone uses the full disk size. Golden images and clones Import an operating system image once into a PVC. Create each VM disk with a DataVolume whose source is that PVC. With a CSI driver that supports snapshots or clones, CDI uses a fast copy. DataImportCron keeps golden images fresh from a registry on a schedule. Uploading For a disk image on your laptop, virtctl image-upload dv my-disk --size=20Gi --image-path=./disk.qcow2 sends it through the CDI upload proxy into a new DataVolume. Incident: The import that never finished Incident A DataVolume stayed in ImportInProgress for an hour. $ kubectl get dv fedora-golden NAME PHASE PROGRESS RESTARTS fedora-golden ImportInProgress 3.20% 6 $ kubectl logs importer-fedora-golden | tail -n 2 E1107 08:14:02 data-processor.go: failed to write: no space left on device The scratch space for converting the qcow2 image was smaller than the image. A StorageClass with enough room for scratch data fixed it, and the import finished in a few minutes. Common mistakes Downloading the same image for every VM Using containerDisk for data that must survive a restart Cloning a golden image across different storage backends without checking speed Not sizing the PVC larger than the image"},"kubevirt/storage-for-vms-access-modes-block-mode-and-snapshots":{"html":"\u003cp>Compute is the easy part of running VMs on Kubernetes. Storage decides what you can actually do with them: migrate, snapshot, clone and survive a node failure.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Access modes and why migration needs ReadWriteMany\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Block mode versus filesystem mode\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What to ask of a CSI driver\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading the LiveMigratable condition\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"access-modes\">Access modes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Mode\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning for a VM\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ReadWriteOnce\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>One node at a time: a running VM cannot move without stopping\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ReadWriteMany\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Several nodes at once: required for live migration with shared storage\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ReadWriteOncePod\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>One pod: not suitable for migration\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>During live migration the source and target virt-launcher pods both attach the disk, so the storage must allow it. Ceph RBD in block mode, many SAN CSI drivers and some NFS setups do. Plain local disks do not.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"volume-mode\">Volume mode\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>volumeMode: Block\u003c/code> hands the raw device to QEMU, which avoids a second file system layer and usually gives better latency. \u003ccode>Filesystem\u003c/code> mode stores a disk image file in a mounted volume; it is simpler but slower and does not support RWX on most block backends. For production VMs prefer Block with an RWX capable driver.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: v1\nkind: PersistentVolumeClaim\nmetadata:\n  name: db1-data\nspec:\n  accessModes: [ReadWriteMany]\n  volumeMode: Block\n  storageClassName: ceph-rbd\n  resources:\n    requests:\n      storage: 100Gi\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">PersistentVolumeClaim\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">db1-data\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">accessModes:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ReadWriteMany\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">volumeMode:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Block\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">storageClassName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ceph-rbd\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">requests:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">storage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">100Gi\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"src&gt;disk;tgt&gt;disk;disk&gt;csi\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Why migration needs a shared disk\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"83.3\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"src\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Source node\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"tgt\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Target node\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"disk\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Shared RWX disk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"csi\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CSI driver\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Source node to Shared RWX disk; 2, Target node to Shared RWX disk; 3, Shared RWX disk to CSI driver.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"src\">\u003cdt>Source node \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Runs the VM and has the disk attached.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A failing node ends the VM if the disk cannot attach elsewhere.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"tgt\">\u003cdt>Target node \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Starts a second launcher and attaches the same disk.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> With RWO the second attach is refused.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"disk\">\u003cdt>Shared RWX disk \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> One block device reachable from both nodes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A slow storage network slows every VM.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"csi\">\u003cdt>CSI driver \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Provides snapshots, clones and expansion.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A driver without snapshots blocks VM backup.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-to-ask-of-the-driver\">What to ask of the driver\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Does it support RWX Block, snapshots, cloning and online expansion? Is there a fast clone path for golden images? Can it be monitored and what is the failure mode when a node loses the storage network?\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-livemigratable-condition\">The LiveMigratable condition\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>KubeVirt marks each VMI with a \u003ccode>LiveMigratable\u003c/code> condition. If it is False the message says why, for example &quot;cannot migrate VMI which does not use a shared ReadWriteMany PVC&quot;. Check it before a maintenance window, not during.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-drain-that-stopped-every-vm\">Incident: The drain that stopped every VM\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Draining a node for maintenance shut down all its VMs.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get vmi db1 -o jsonpath='{.status.conditions[?(@.type==&quot;LiveMigratable&quot;)].message}'\nkubectl get pvc db1-data -o jsonpath='{.spec.accessModes}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get vmi db1 -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.status.conditions[?(@.type==&quot;LiveMigratable&quot;)].message}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">cannot migrate VMI which does not use a shared ReadWriteMany PVC\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pvc db1-data -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.accessModes}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[&quot;ReadWriteOnce&quot;]\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The disks were created on a ReadWriteOnce class, so KubeVirt could not migrate them during the drain. The team moved the disks to an RWX block class and rehearsed a drain before the next window.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choosing ReadWriteOnce and expecting live migration\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using filesystem mode for latency sensitive databases\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not testing a drain before maintenance\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Picking a CSI driver without snapshot support\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"access-modes","text":"Access modes"},{"id":"volume-mode","text":"Volume mode"},{"id":"what-to-ask-of-the-driver","text":"What to ask of the driver"},{"id":"the-livemigratable-condition","text":"The LiveMigratable condition"},{"id":"incident-the-drain-that-stopped-every-vm","text":"Incident: The drain that stopped every VM"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"KubeVirt storage","u":"https://kubevirt.io/user-guide/storage/"},{"t":"Kubernetes storage classes","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/storage-classes/"},{"t":"Volume snapshots","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/volume-snapshots/"}],"text":"Compute is the easy part of running VMs on Kubernetes. Storage decides what you can actually do with them: migrate, snapshot, clone and survive a node failure. What you will learn Access modes and why migration needs ReadWriteMany Block mode versus filesystem mode What to ask of a CSI driver Reading the LiveMigratable condition Access modes Mode Meaning for a VM ReadWriteOnce One node at a time: a running VM cannot move without stopping ReadWriteMany Several nodes at once: required for live migration with shared storage ReadWriteOncePod One pod: not suitable for migration During live migration the source and target virt-launcher pods both attach the disk, so the storage must allow it. Ceph RBD in block mode, many SAN CSI drivers and some NFS setups do. Plain local disks do not. Volume mode volumeMode: Block hands the raw device to QEMU, which avoids a second file system layer and usually gives better latency. Filesystem mode stores a disk image file in a mounted volume; it is simpler but slower and does not support RWX on most block backends. For production VMs prefer Block with an RWX capable driver. apiVersion: v1 kind: PersistentVolumeClaim metadata: name: db1-data spec: accessModes: [ ReadWriteMany ] volumeMode: Block storageClassName: ceph-rbd resources: requests: storage: 100Gi Why migration needs a shared disk Flow in order: 1, Source node to Shared RWX disk; 2, Target node to Shared RWX disk; 3, Shared RWX disk to CSI driver. Service Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Source node Service What it does: Runs the VM and has the disk attached. If it fails or is weak: A failing node ends the VM if the disk cannot attach elsewhere. Target node Service What it does: Starts a second launcher and attaches the same disk. If it fails or is weak: With RWO the second attach is refused. Shared RWX disk Data store What it does: One block device reachable from both nodes. If it fails or is weak: A slow storage network slows every VM. CSI driver Data store What it does: Provides snapshots, clones and expansion. If it fails or is weak: A driver without snapshots blocks VM backup. What to ask of the driver Does it support RWX Block, snapshots, cloning and online expansion? Is there a fast clone path for golden images? Can it be monitored and what is the failure mode when a node loses the storage network? The LiveMigratable condition KubeVirt marks each VMI with a LiveMigratable condition. If it is False the message says why, for example \"cannot migrate VMI which does not use a shared ReadWriteMany PVC\". Check it before a maintenance window, not during. Incident: The drain that stopped every VM Incident Draining a node for maintenance shut down all its VMs. $ kubectl get vmi db1 -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.status.conditions[?(@.type==\"LiveMigratable\")].message}&#x27; cannot migrate VMI which does not use a shared ReadWriteMany PVC $ kubectl get pvc db1-data -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.spec.accessModes}&#x27; [\"ReadWriteOnce\"] The disks were created on a ReadWriteOnce class, so KubeVirt could not migrate them during the drain. The team moved the disks to an RWX block class and rehearsed a drain before the next window. Common mistakes Choosing ReadWriteOnce and expecting live migration Using filesystem mode for latency sensitive databases Not testing a drain before maintenance Picking a CSI driver without snapshot support"},"kubevirt/vm-networking-masquerade-bridge-and-multus":{"html":"\u003cp>Networking is where VMs differ most from containers. Guests expect network interfaces, MAC addresses and often fixed IPs. KubeVirt maps those wishes onto Kubernetes networking.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The pod network and masquerade binding\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Secondary networks with Multus and bridge\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Exposing a VM with Services\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Live migration and addresses\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-pod-network\">The pod network\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every VM can attach to the default pod network. With \u003cstrong>masquerade\u003c/strong> binding, the guest gets a private address and KubeVirt NATs traffic to the pod&#39;s IP. Pods and Services can then reach the VM, and the VM reaches the cluster and the internet. Masquerade also supports live migration, because the guest keeps its private address while the pod address changes. The older \u003ccode>bridge\u003c/code> binding on the pod network is limited and not recommended.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"      networks:\n        - name: default\n          pod: {}\n        - name: vlan100\n          multus:\n            networkName: vlan100-bridge\n      domain:\n        devices:\n          interfaces:\n            - name: default\n              masquerade: {}\n            - name: vlan100\n              bridge: {}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">networks:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">default\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">pod:\u003c/span> {}\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">vlan100\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">multus:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">networkName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">vlan100-bridge\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">domain:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">devices:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">interfaces:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">default\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">masquerade:\u003c/span> {}\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">vlan100\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">bridge:\u003c/span> {}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"secondary-networks\">Secondary networks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003ccode>NetworkAttachmentDefinition\u003c/code> describes an extra network, for example a Linux bridge connected to VLAN 100 on the node. Multus creates the second interface. With \u003ccode>bridge\u003c/code> binding the guest sees a real L2 network and can keep its own IP or get DHCP from the VLAN. Migration across nodes then needs the same bridge and VLAN on every node.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"pod&gt;guest;vlan&gt;guest;svc&gt;pod;vlan&gt;phys\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">One VM on two networks\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"83.3\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"16.7\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"guest\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Guest OS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pod\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pod network (masquerade)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"vlan\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">VLAN 100 bridge\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"svc\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Service / Ingress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"phys\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Physical network\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Pod network (masquerade) to Guest OS; 2, VLAN 100 bridge to Guest OS; 3, Service / Ingress to Pod network (masquerade); 4, VLAN 100 bridge to Physical network.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"guest\">\u003cdt>Guest OS \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sees two NICs, eth0 and eth1.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong interface names in cloud-init break first boot.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pod\">\u003cdt>Pod network (masquerade) \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> NATed address, reachable by Services and pods.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> The guest IP is private, so inbound needs a Service.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"vlan\">\u003cdt>VLAN 100 bridge \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Real L2 network with its own addresses.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> The bridge must exist on every node for migration.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"svc\">\u003cdt>Service / Ingress \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Exposes VM ports to the cluster or outside.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A selector must match the VMI labels.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"phys\">\u003cdt>Physical network \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Carries the VLAN to the data centre.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A missing trunk port blocks all VLAN traffic.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"services-and-ports\">Services and ports\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>virtctl expose vm web01 --port=80 --name=web01-svc\u003c/code> creates a Service that selects the VMI. Network policies apply to the pod network but not to a Multus secondary interface, so secondary networks need their own controls.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-vlan-that-only-worked-on-one-node\">Incident: The VLAN that only worked on one node\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A VM reached its VLAN, but after a migration it lost the network.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get vmi web01 -o wide\nssh node3 'ip link show br100'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get vmi web01 -o wide\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME    PHASE     NODENAME\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web01   Running   node3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ssh node3 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ip link show br100&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Device &quot;br100&quot; does not exist.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Only two of six nodes had the br100 bridge that the NetworkAttachmentDefinition named. The VM migrated to node3, which had no bridge, and lost its VLAN. The bridge was added to every node by the node configuration tool.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using pod-network bridge binding instead of masquerade\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting the secondary bridge must exist on all nodes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expecting NetworkPolicy to cover Multus interfaces\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Hard-coding guest IPs on the pod network\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-pod-network","text":"The pod network"},{"id":"secondary-networks","text":"Secondary networks"},{"id":"services-and-ports","text":"Services and ports"},{"id":"incident-the-vlan-that-only-worked-on-one-node","text":"Incident: The VLAN that only worked on one node"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"KubeVirt interfaces and networks","u":"https://kubevirt.io/user-guide/network/interfaces_and_networks/"},{"t":"Multus CNI","u":"https://github.com/k8snetworkplumbingwg/multus-cni"},{"t":"Kubernetes services","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/"}],"text":"Networking is where VMs differ most from containers. Guests expect network interfaces, MAC addresses and often fixed IPs. KubeVirt maps those wishes onto Kubernetes networking. What you will learn The pod network and masquerade binding Secondary networks with Multus and bridge Exposing a VM with Services Live migration and addresses The pod network Every VM can attach to the default pod network. With masquerade binding, the guest gets a private address and KubeVirt NATs traffic to the pod&#39;s IP. Pods and Services can then reach the VM, and the VM reaches the cluster and the internet. Masquerade also supports live migration, because the guest keeps its private address while the pod address changes. The older bridge binding on the pod network is limited and not recommended. networks: - name: default pod: {} - name: vlan100 multus: networkName: vlan100-bridge domain: devices: interfaces: - name: default masquerade: {} - name: vlan100 bridge: {} Secondary networks A NetworkAttachmentDefinition describes an extra network, for example a Linux bridge connected to VLAN 100 on the node. Multus creates the second interface. With bridge binding the guest sees a real L2 network and can keep its own IP or get DHCP from the VLAN. Migration across nodes then needs the same bridge and VLAN on every node. One VM on two networks Flow in order: 1, Pod network (masquerade) to Guest OS; 2, VLAN 100 bridge to Guest OS; 3, Service / Ingress to Pod network (masquerade); 4, VLAN 100 bridge to Physical network. Service Network edge Outside party Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Guest OS Service What it does: Sees two NICs, eth0 and eth1. If it fails or is weak: Wrong interface names in cloud-init break first boot. Pod network (masquerade) Network edge What it does: NATed address, reachable by Services and pods. If it fails or is weak: The guest IP is private, so inbound needs a Service. VLAN 100 bridge Network edge What it does: Real L2 network with its own addresses. If it fails or is weak: The bridge must exist on every node for migration. Service / Ingress Network edge What it does: Exposes VM ports to the cluster or outside. If it fails or is weak: A selector must match the VMI labels. Physical network Outside party What it does: Carries the VLAN to the data centre. If it fails or is weak: A missing trunk port blocks all VLAN traffic. Services and ports virtctl expose vm web01 --port=80 --name=web01-svc creates a Service that selects the VMI. Network policies apply to the pod network but not to a Multus secondary interface, so secondary networks need their own controls. Incident: The VLAN that only worked on one node Incident A VM reached its VLAN, but after a migration it lost the network. $ kubectl get vmi web01 -o wide NAME PHASE NODENAME web01 Running node3 $ ssh node3 &#x27;ip link show br100&#x27; Device \"br100\" does not exist. Only two of six nodes had the br100 bridge that the NetworkAttachmentDefinition named. The VM migrated to node3, which had no bridge, and lost its VLAN. The bridge was added to every node by the node configuration tool. Common mistakes Using pod-network bridge binding instead of masquerade Forgetting the secondary bridge must exist on all nodes Expecting NetworkPolicy to cover Multus interfaces Hard-coding guest IPs on the pod network"},"kubevirt/cloud-init-instance-types-and-vm-image-pipelines":{"html":"\u003cp>A VM you configured by hand is a pet. A VM built from an image, an instance type and a cloud-init file can be recreated at any time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Cloud-init volumes and what they can set\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Instance types and preferences\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Building and testing golden images\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping secrets out of userData\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cloud-init\">Cloud-init\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Most Linux cloud images run cloud-init at first boot. In KubeVirt, a \u003ccode>cloudInitNoCloud\u003c/code> volume carries the data as a small disk. Typical settings are a user, an SSH key, package installs and commands.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"      volumes:\n        - name: cloudinit\n          cloudInitNoCloud:\n            userData: |\n              #cloud-config\n              users:\n                - name: ops\n                  ssh_authorized_keys:\n                    - ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3Nza... ops@example\n              package_update: true\n              packages:\n                - qemu-guest-agent\n              runcmd:\n                - systemctl enable --now qemu-guest-agent\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">volumes:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">cloudinit\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cloudInitNoCloud:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">userData:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">|\n              #cloud-config\n              users:\n                - name: ops\n                  ssh_authorized_keys:\n                    - ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3Nza... ops@example\n              package_update: true\n              packages:\n                - qemu-guest-agent\n              runcmd:\n                - systemctl enable --now qemu-guest-agent\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Install \u003ccode>qemu-guest-agent\u003c/code> in every image. KubeVirt uses it for the guest IP, clean shutdown and filesystem freeze for snapshots.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"instance-types-and-preferences\">Instance types and preferences\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: instancetype.kubevirt.io/v1beta1\nkind: VirtualMachineClusterInstancetype\nmetadata:\n  name: medium\nspec:\n  cpu:\n    guest: 4\n  memory:\n    guest: 8Gi\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">instancetype.kubevirt.io/v1beta1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">VirtualMachineClusterInstancetype\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">medium\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cpu:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">guest:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">4\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">memory:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">guest:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">8Gi\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A VM references it with \u003ccode>spec.instancetype.name: medium\u003c/code>. A \u003ccode>VirtualMachinePreference\u003c/code> sets device defaults such as the disk bus and network model. Changing sizes then means choosing another type, not editing CPU and memory in every VM.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"image&gt;vm;type&gt;vm;pref&gt;vm;ci&gt;vm\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A VM assembled from reusable parts\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"image\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Golden image\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"type\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Instance type\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pref\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Preference\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ci\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cloud-init data\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"vm\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">VirtualMachine\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Golden image to VirtualMachine; 2, Instance type to VirtualMachine; 3, Preference to VirtualMachine; 4, Cloud-init data to VirtualMachine.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"image\">\u003cdt>Golden image \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The operating system disk, built and tested once.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An untested image breaks every VM cloned from it.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"type\">\u003cdt>Instance type \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> CPU and memory such as medium.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Changing it affects VMs created later, not running ones.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pref\">\u003cdt>Preference \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Disk bus, NIC model and other device defaults.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A wrong bus can stop Windows booting.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ci\">\u003cdt>Cloud-init data \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Users, keys and first-boot commands.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Secrets in userData are readable by anyone who can read the VM.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"vm\">\u003cdt>VirtualMachine \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The result: a consistent, repeatable VM.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Drift returns if people log in and change it by hand.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"image-pipelines\">Image pipelines\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Build images with a tool such as Packer or a CI job: start from a vendor cloud image, apply updates, install the guest agent, test boot in a throwaway VM, then publish the disk to a registry or PVC. Version the images and keep the last few.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"secrets\">Secrets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Do not put passwords or tokens in \u003ccode>userData\u003c/code>. Use a Secret referenced by \u003ccode>userDataSecretRef\u003c/code>, or fetch secrets at runtime from a manager such as Vault.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-fifty-different-sizes\">Incident: Fifty different sizes\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A capacity review found VMs with 3, 5 and 7 CPUs and no pattern.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get vm -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,CPU:.spec.template.spec.domain.cpu.cores\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get vm -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,CPU:.spec.template.spec.domain.cpu.cores\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME    CPU\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">app01   3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">app02   5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">app03   7\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Every VM was created by copying a nearby manifest and editing numbers. The team defined small, medium and large instance types, migrated VMs to them at their next restart, and sizing reviews became a single table.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Building images by logging in and installing things\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving qemu-guest-agent out of the image\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting secrets in userData\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expecting an instance type change to resize a running VM\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"cloud-init","text":"Cloud-init"},{"id":"instance-types-and-preferences","text":"Instance types and preferences"},{"id":"image-pipelines","text":"Image pipelines"},{"id":"secrets","text":"Secrets"},{"id":"incident-fifty-different-sizes","text":"Incident: Fifty different sizes"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Startup scripts and cloud-init","u":"https://kubevirt.io/user-guide/user_workloads/startup_scripts/"},{"t":"Instance types and preferences","u":"https://kubevirt.io/user-guide/user_workloads/instancetypes/"},{"t":"cloud-init documentation","u":"https://cloudinit.readthedocs.io/"}],"text":"A VM you configured by hand is a pet. A VM built from an image, an instance type and a cloud-init file can be recreated at any time. What you will learn Cloud-init volumes and what they can set Instance types and preferences Building and testing golden images Keeping secrets out of userData Cloud-init Most Linux cloud images run cloud-init at first boot. In KubeVirt, a cloudInitNoCloud volume carries the data as a small disk. Typical settings are a user, an SSH key, package installs and commands. volumes: - name: cloudinit cloudInitNoCloud: userData: | #cloud-config users: - name: ops ssh_authorized_keys: - ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3Nza... ops@example package_update: true packages: - qemu-guest-agent runcmd: - systemctl enable --now qemu-guest-agent Install qemu-guest-agent in every image. KubeVirt uses it for the guest IP, clean shutdown and filesystem freeze for snapshots. Instance types and preferences apiVersion: instancetype.kubevirt.io/v1beta1 kind: VirtualMachineClusterInstancetype metadata: name: medium spec: cpu: guest: 4 memory: guest: 8Gi A VM references it with spec.instancetype.name: medium . A VirtualMachinePreference sets device defaults such as the disk bus and network model. Changing sizes then means choosing another type, not editing CPU and memory in every VM. A VM assembled from reusable parts Flow in order: 1, Golden image to VirtualMachine; 2, Instance type to VirtualMachine; 3, Preference to VirtualMachine; 4, Cloud-init data to VirtualMachine. Data store Step Security Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Golden image Data store What it does: The operating system disk, built and tested once. If it fails or is weak: An untested image breaks every VM cloned from it. Instance type Step What it does: CPU and memory such as medium. If it fails or is weak: Changing it affects VMs created later, not running ones. Preference Step What it does: Disk bus, NIC model and other device defaults. If it fails or is weak: A wrong bus can stop Windows booting. Cloud-init data Security What it does: Users, keys and first-boot commands. If it fails or is weak: Secrets in userData are readable by anyone who can read the VM. VirtualMachine Service What it does: The result: a consistent, repeatable VM. If it fails or is weak: Drift returns if people log in and change it by hand. Image pipelines Build images with a tool such as Packer or a CI job: start from a vendor cloud image, apply updates, install the guest agent, test boot in a throwaway VM, then publish the disk to a registry or PVC. Version the images and keep the last few. Secrets Do not put passwords or tokens in userData . Use a Secret referenced by userDataSecretRef , or fetch secrets at runtime from a manager such as Vault. Incident: Fifty different sizes Incident A capacity review found VMs with 3, 5 and 7 CPUs and no pattern. $ kubectl get vm -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,CPU:.spec.template.spec.domain.cpu.cores NAME CPU app01 3 app02 5 app03 7 Every VM was created by copying a nearby manifest and editing numbers. The team defined small, medium and large instance types, migrated VMs to them at their next restart, and sizing reviews became a single table. Common mistakes Building images by logging in and installing things Leaving qemu-guest-agent out of the image Putting secrets in userData Expecting an instance type change to resize a running VM"},"kubevirt/live-migration-node-drains-and-eviction":{"html":"\u003cp>Live migration is the feature that makes maintenance painless. It also has the most ways to fail, so it pays to know how it works and how to read its failures.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How live migration works step by step\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Eviction strategy and node drains\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Migration policies and limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why a migration fails or never finishes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-works\">How it works\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>KubeVirt starts a second virt-launcher pod on the target node and attaches the same disks. QEMU copies the guest&#39;s memory pages to the target while the VM keeps running, re-copying pages that change. When the remaining difference is small, the VM is paused for a brief moment, the last state is sent, and the target takes over.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"src&gt;net;net&gt;tgt;src&gt;disk;tgt&gt;disk;ctrl&gt;tgt\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A live migration\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"83.3\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"src\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Source virt-launcher\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"net\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Migration network\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"disk\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Shared RWX disk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"tgt\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Target virt-launcher\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ctrl\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">virt-controller\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Source virt-launcher to Migration network; 2, Migration network to Target virt-launcher; 3, Source virt-launcher to Shared RWX disk; 4, Target virt-launcher to Shared RWX disk; 5, virt-controller to Target virt-launcher.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"src\">\u003cdt>Source virt-launcher \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Runs the VM and sends memory pages.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A busy VM can change memory faster than it is copied.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"net\">\u003cdt>Migration network \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Carries the memory copy between the nodes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A slow or shared link stretches the migration.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"disk\">\u003cdt>Shared RWX disk \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Both pods attach the same disk.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> RWO storage makes migration impossible.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"tgt\">\u003cdt>Target virt-launcher \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Receives pages and takes over after a short pause.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing CPU features on the target abort the move.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ctrl\">\u003cdt>virt-controller \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Creates the target pod and tracks progress.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Cluster limits queue extra migrations.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"starting-and-draining\">Starting and draining\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virtctl migrate web01\nkubectl get vmim\nkubectl drain node2 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">virtctl migrate web01\nkubectl get vmim\nkubectl drain node2 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Set \u003ccode>evictionStrategy: LiveMigrate\u003c/code> on the VM, or as a cluster default. A drain then creates migrations for VMs on the node instead of shutting them down. VMs that cannot migrate block the drain, which is safer than killing them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"migration-policies\">Migration policies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: migrations.kubevirt.io/v1alpha1\nkind: MigrationPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: db-policy\nspec:\n  allowAutoConverge: true\n  bandwidthPerMigration: 512Mi\n  completionTimeoutPerGiB: 800\n  selectors:\n    virtualMachineInstanceSelector:\n      workload: database\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">migrations.kubevirt.io/v1alpha1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">MigrationPolicy\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">db-policy\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">allowAutoConverge:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">bandwidthPerMigration:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">512Mi\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">completionTimeoutPerGiB:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">800\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">selectors:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">virtualMachineInstanceSelector:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">workload:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">database\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Auto-converge slows the guest&#39;s CPU so pages stop changing as fast. Post-copy switches early and fetches the rest on demand, which is faster but risky if the network fails. Cluster limits such as parallel migrations per node stop a drain from saturating the network.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-they-fail\">Why they fail\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Storage not shared, CPU model differing between nodes, a passthrough device (GPU) attached, a node selector that matches only one node, a network too slow for the dirty rate, or a timeout shorter than the copy needs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-drain-that-would-not-finish\">Incident: The drain that would not finish\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A node drain hung for an hour with one VM left.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get vmim -o wide\nkubectl logs virt-launcher-db1 | tail -n 1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get vmim -o wide\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME           PHASE     VMI\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">kubevirt-m1    Running   db1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs virt-launcher-db1 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -n 1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">migration is not progressing, dirty rate is higher than transfer rate\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The database dirtied memory faster than the 64 MiB per second bandwidth limit could copy. A policy with auto-converge and a higher limit let the migration complete in minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Draining nodes before checking LiveMigratable\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving a low bandwidth limit on busy VMs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Passing through a device and expecting migration\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running the first migration test during an outage\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"how-it-works","text":"How it works"},{"id":"starting-and-draining","text":"Starting and draining"},{"id":"migration-policies","text":"Migration policies"},{"id":"why-they-fail","text":"Why they fail"},{"id":"incident-the-drain-that-would-not-finish","text":"Incident: The drain that would not finish"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Live migration","u":"https://kubevirt.io/user-guide/compute/live_migration/"},{"t":"Migration policies","u":"https://kubevirt.io/user-guide/cluster_admin/migration_policies/"},{"t":"Safely drain a node","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/safely-drain-node/"}],"text":"Live migration is the feature that makes maintenance painless. It also has the most ways to fail, so it pays to know how it works and how to read its failures. What you will learn How live migration works step by step Eviction strategy and node drains Migration policies and limits Why a migration fails or never finishes How it works KubeVirt starts a second virt-launcher pod on the target node and attaches the same disks. QEMU copies the guest&#39;s memory pages to the target while the VM keeps running, re-copying pages that change. When the remaining difference is small, the VM is paused for a brief moment, the last state is sent, and the target takes over. A live migration Flow in order: 1, Source virt-launcher to Migration network; 2, Migration network to Target virt-launcher; 3, Source virt-launcher to Shared RWX disk; 4, Target virt-launcher to Shared RWX disk; 5, virt-controller to Target virt-launcher. Service Network edge Data store Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Source virt-launcher Service What it does: Runs the VM and sends memory pages. If it fails or is weak: A busy VM can change memory faster than it is copied. Migration network Network edge What it does: Carries the memory copy between the nodes. If it fails or is weak: A slow or shared link stretches the migration. Shared RWX disk Data store What it does: Both pods attach the same disk. If it fails or is weak: RWO storage makes migration impossible. Target virt-launcher Service What it does: Receives pages and takes over after a short pause. If it fails or is weak: Missing CPU features on the target abort the move. virt-controller Step What it does: Creates the target pod and tracks progress. If it fails or is weak: Cluster limits queue extra migrations. Starting and draining virtctl migrate web01 kubectl get vmim kubectl drain node2 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data Set evictionStrategy: LiveMigrate on the VM, or as a cluster default. A drain then creates migrations for VMs on the node instead of shutting them down. VMs that cannot migrate block the drain, which is safer than killing them. Migration policies apiVersion: migrations.kubevirt.io/v1alpha1 kind: MigrationPolicy metadata: name: db-policy spec: allowAutoConverge: true bandwidthPerMigration: 512Mi completionTimeoutPerGiB: 800 selectors: virtualMachineInstanceSelector: workload: database Auto-converge slows the guest&#39;s CPU so pages stop changing as fast. Post-copy switches early and fetches the rest on demand, which is faster but risky if the network fails. Cluster limits such as parallel migrations per node stop a drain from saturating the network. Why they fail Storage not shared, CPU model differing between nodes, a passthrough device (GPU) attached, a node selector that matches only one node, a network too slow for the dirty rate, or a timeout shorter than the copy needs. Incident: The drain that would not finish Incident A node drain hung for an hour with one VM left. $ kubectl get vmim -o wide NAME PHASE VMI kubevirt-m1 Running db1 $ kubectl logs virt-launcher-db1 | tail -n 1 migration is not progressing, dirty rate is higher than transfer rate The database dirtied memory faster than the 64 MiB per second bandwidth limit could copy. A policy with auto-converge and a higher limit let the migration complete in minutes. Common mistakes Draining nodes before checking LiveMigratable Leaving a low bandwidth limit on busy VMs Passing through a device and expecting migration Running the first migration test during an outage"},"kubevirt/cpu-memory-hugepages-and-vm-performance":{"html":"\u003cp>Most VM performance problems come from sizing and contention, not from the hypervisor. Know what the pod limits mean, then decide whether pinning or hugepages are worth the loss of flexibility.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Requests, limits and memory overhead\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dedicated CPUs and NUMA\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Hugepages\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Overcommit and noisy neighbours\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"requests-limits-and-overhead\">Requests, limits and overhead\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The virt-launcher pod needs memory for the guest plus overhead for QEMU, libvirt and device emulation. KubeVirt adds an overhead to the pod request automatically, and it grows with CPU count and devices. A pod limit equal to guest memory causes out-of-memory kills. A VM with CPU limits can be throttled even when the node is idle.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"dedicated-cpus\">Dedicated CPUs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"      domain:\n        cpu:\n          cores: 4\n          dedicatedCpuPlacement: true\n        resources:\n          requests:\n            memory: 8Gi\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">domain:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cpu:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cores:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">4\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">dedicatedCpuPlacement:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">requests:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">8Gi\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>With the kubelet CPU manager set to \u003ccode>static\u003c/code> and a Guaranteed QoS pod (requests equal limits), the VM gets exclusive cores. That removes scheduling jitter from other pods. The cost is that those cores are no longer shared, so the node holds fewer VMs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"guest&gt;pod;over&gt;pod;cpu&gt;pod;huge&gt;pod\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">What the VM pod needs from the node\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"guest\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Guest memory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"over\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">QEMU and libvirt overhead\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pod\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pod request and limit\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cpu\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Dedicated CPUs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"huge\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Hugepages\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Guest memory to Pod request and limit; 2, QEMU and libvirt overhead to Pod request and limit; 3, Dedicated CPUs to Pod request and limit; 4, Hugepages to Pod request and limit.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"guest\">\u003cdt>Guest memory \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The memory the guest operating system sees.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If the app needs more, the guest swaps or kills processes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"over\">\u003cdt>QEMU and libvirt overhead \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Extra memory for emulation, page tables and devices.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing from the limit, it causes OOM kills.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pod\">\u003cdt>Pod request and limit \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The sum the scheduler and kubelet enforce.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A limit below the sum kills the container.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cpu\">\u003cdt>Dedicated CPUs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Exclusive cores from the static CPU manager.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Needs Guaranteed QoS and integer CPU counts.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"huge\">\u003cdt>Hugepages \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Large memory pages reserved on the node.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Pages must be preallocated or the VM will not start.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"hugepages\">Hugepages\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>memory.hugepages.pageSize: 1Gi\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>2Mi\u003c/code> backs guest memory with large pages, reducing translation overhead for databases and low latency software. The node must have hugepages reserved at boot or by configuration.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"overcommit-and-noisy-neighbours\">Overcommit and noisy neighbours\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Requests lower than limits pack more VMs per node, but a busy neighbour can steal CPU. Watch CPU steal and throttling metrics, keep critical VMs on Guaranteed QoS, and separate noisy workloads with node pools.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-vm-killed-by-its-own-pod\">Incident: The VM killed by its own pod\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A healthy VM restarted overnight with OOMKilled.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl describe pod virt-launcher-app01 | grep -A3 'Last State'\nkubectl get pod virt-launcher-app01 -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].resources.limits.memory}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl describe pod virt-launcher-app01 | grep -A3 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Last State&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    Last State:  Terminated\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">      Reason:    OOMKilled\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pod virt-launcher-app01 -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.containers[0].resources.limits.memory}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">8Gi\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The guest had 8 GiB and the pod limit was also 8 GiB. QEMU overhead pushed the container over its limit. Raising the pod memory limit above the guest size stopped the kills.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Setting the pod memory limit equal to the guest memory\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pinning CPUs for every VM\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using hugepages without reserving them first\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring CPU throttling on VMs with CPU limits\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"requests-limits-and-overhead","text":"Requests, limits and overhead"},{"id":"dedicated-cpus","text":"Dedicated CPUs"},{"id":"hugepages","text":"Hugepages"},{"id":"overcommit-and-noisy-neighbours","text":"Overcommit and noisy neighbours"},{"id":"incident-the-vm-killed-by-its-own-pod","text":"Incident: The VM killed by its own pod"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Dedicated CPU resources","u":"https://kubevirt.io/user-guide/compute/dedicated_cpu_resources/"},{"t":"Hugepages","u":"https://kubevirt.io/user-guide/compute/hugepages/"},{"t":"Kubernetes CPU management policies","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/cpu-management-policies/"}],"text":"Most VM performance problems come from sizing and contention, not from the hypervisor. Know what the pod limits mean, then decide whether pinning or hugepages are worth the loss of flexibility. What you will learn Requests, limits and memory overhead Dedicated CPUs and NUMA Hugepages Overcommit and noisy neighbours Requests, limits and overhead The virt-launcher pod needs memory for the guest plus overhead for QEMU, libvirt and device emulation. KubeVirt adds an overhead to the pod request automatically, and it grows with CPU count and devices. A pod limit equal to guest memory causes out-of-memory kills. A VM with CPU limits can be throttled even when the node is idle. Dedicated CPUs domain: cpu: cores: 4 dedicatedCpuPlacement: true resources: requests: memory: 8Gi With the kubelet CPU manager set to static and a Guaranteed QoS pod (requests equal limits), the VM gets exclusive cores. That removes scheduling jitter from other pods. The cost is that those cores are no longer shared, so the node holds fewer VMs. What the VM pod needs from the node Flow in order: 1, Guest memory to Pod request and limit; 2, QEMU and libvirt overhead to Pod request and limit; 3, Dedicated CPUs to Pod request and limit; 4, Hugepages to Pod request and limit. Data store Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Guest memory Data store What it does: The memory the guest operating system sees. If it fails or is weak: If the app needs more, the guest swaps or kills processes. QEMU and libvirt overhead Step What it does: Extra memory for emulation, page tables and devices. If it fails or is weak: Missing from the limit, it causes OOM kills. Pod request and limit Service What it does: The sum the scheduler and kubelet enforce. If it fails or is weak: A limit below the sum kills the container. Dedicated CPUs Service What it does: Exclusive cores from the static CPU manager. If it fails or is weak: Needs Guaranteed QoS and integer CPU counts. Hugepages Data store What it does: Large memory pages reserved on the node. If it fails or is weak: Pages must be preallocated or the VM will not start. Hugepages memory.hugepages.pageSize: 1Gi or 2Mi backs guest memory with large pages, reducing translation overhead for databases and low latency software. The node must have hugepages reserved at boot or by configuration. Overcommit and noisy neighbours Requests lower than limits pack more VMs per node, but a busy neighbour can steal CPU. Watch CPU steal and throttling metrics, keep critical VMs on Guaranteed QoS, and separate noisy workloads with node pools. Incident: The VM killed by its own pod Incident A healthy VM restarted overnight with OOMKilled. $ kubectl describe pod virt-launcher-app01 | grep -A3 &#x27;Last State&#x27; Last State: Terminated Reason: OOMKilled $ kubectl get pod virt-launcher-app01 -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.spec.containers[0].resources.limits.memory}&#x27; 8Gi The guest had 8 GiB and the pod limit was also 8 GiB. QEMU overhead pushed the container over its limit. Raising the pod memory limit above the guest size stopped the kills. Common mistakes Setting the pod memory limit equal to the guest memory Pinning CPUs for every VM Using hugepages without reserving them first Ignoring CPU throttling on VMs with CPU limits"},"kubevirt/vm-snapshots-backup-and-restore":{"html":"\u003cp>Backing up a VM is not the same as backing up a container. The disk holds a running operating system with open files, so how you take the copy matters as much as where you store it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Snapshots with the guest agent\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restore and clone\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Backups that leave the cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Testing a restore\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"snapshots\">Snapshots\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: snapshot.kubevirt.io/v1beta1\nkind: VirtualMachineSnapshot\nmetadata:\n  name: web01-before-upgrade\nspec:\n  source:\n    apiGroup: kubevirt.io\n    kind: VirtualMachine\n    name: web01\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">snapshot.kubevirt.io/v1beta1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">VirtualMachineSnapshot\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web01-before-upgrade\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">source:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiGroup:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">kubevirt.io\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">VirtualMachine\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web01\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>KubeVirt creates a CSI VolumeSnapshot for each disk. With \u003ccode>qemu-guest-agent\u003c/code> running, it asks the guest to freeze its file systems briefly, so the snapshot is consistent. Without the agent, the snapshot is only crash consistent, like pulling the power. Applications such as databases need pre and post hooks for a clean state.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"restore\">Restore\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003ccode>VirtualMachineRestore\u003c/code> object points a VM at a snapshot. The VM must be stopped, and its disks are replaced from the snapshot. To keep the original, create a new VM from the snapshot instead. The snapshot also records the VM definition, so the configuration returns with the data.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"vm&gt;agent;agent&gt;snap;snap&gt;back;snap&gt;rest;back&gt;rest\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Snapshot versus backup\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"vm\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Running VM\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"agent\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Guest agent freeze\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"snap\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CSI snapshot\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"back\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Backup on object storage\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rest\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Restore\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Running VM to Guest agent freeze; 2, Guest agent freeze to CSI snapshot; 3, CSI snapshot to Backup on object storage; 4, CSI snapshot to Restore; 5, Backup on object storage to Restore.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"vm\">\u003cdt>Running VM \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A guest with open files and memory state.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Copying the disk while it runs gives a torn copy.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"agent\">\u003cdt>Guest agent freeze \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Pauses writes for a moment.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without it the snapshot is crash consistent.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"snap\">\u003cdt>CSI snapshot \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A fast point in time copy on the same storage.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A storage failure removes disk and snapshot together.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"back\">\u003cdt>Backup on object storage \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A copy in another place, made by Velero or an export.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An untested backup is only a hope.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rest\">\u003cdt>Restore \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Returns the VM from a snapshot or backup.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Restoring over a running VM can overwrite newer data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"backups-that-leave-the-cluster\">Backups that leave the cluster\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Velero with the KubeVirt plugin backs up VM objects and, with a CSI data mover, copies volume snapshot data to object storage. \u003ccode>VirtualMachineExport\u003c/code> serves a disk over HTTP for ad hoc copies. Schedule backups, set retention, and keep at least one copy in another failure domain.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"test-restores\">Test restores\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Schedule a restore into a test namespace each month and boot the VM. Check application data, not only that the VM starts.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-backup-on-the-same-pool\">Incident: The backup on the same pool\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A storage pool failed and both the disks and snapshots were lost.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get volumesnapshotcontent -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,POOL:.status.snapshotHandle\nceph health\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get volumesnapshotcontent -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,POOL:.status.snapshotHandle\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME             POOL\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">snapcontent-1   ceph-pool-a/csi-snap-77\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ceph health\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">HEALTH_ERR 1 pool inconsistent\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Every snapshot lived in the same Ceph pool as the disks it protected. The team added Velero backups to object storage in another site and a monthly restore test.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating snapshots as backups\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Taking snapshots without the guest agent for databases\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never testing a restore\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restoring over a running VM without a plan\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"snapshots","text":"Snapshots"},{"id":"restore","text":"Restore"},{"id":"backups-that-leave-the-cluster","text":"Backups that leave the cluster"},{"id":"test-restores","text":"Test restores"},{"id":"incident-the-backup-on-the-same-pool","text":"Incident: The backup on the same pool"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Snapshot and restore","u":"https://kubevirt.io/user-guide/storage/snapshot_restore_api/"},{"t":"Velero documentation","u":"https://velero.io/docs/"},{"t":"Volume snapshots","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/volume-snapshots/"}],"text":"Backing up a VM is not the same as backing up a container. The disk holds a running operating system with open files, so how you take the copy matters as much as where you store it. What you will learn Snapshots with the guest agent Restore and clone Backups that leave the cluster Testing a restore Snapshots apiVersion: snapshot.kubevirt.io/v1beta1 kind: VirtualMachineSnapshot metadata: name: web01-before-upgrade spec: source: apiGroup: kubevirt.io kind: VirtualMachine name: web01 KubeVirt creates a CSI VolumeSnapshot for each disk. With qemu-guest-agent running, it asks the guest to freeze its file systems briefly, so the snapshot is consistent. Without the agent, the snapshot is only crash consistent, like pulling the power. Applications such as databases need pre and post hooks for a clean state. Restore A VirtualMachineRestore object points a VM at a snapshot. The VM must be stopped, and its disks are replaced from the snapshot. To keep the original, create a new VM from the snapshot instead. The snapshot also records the VM definition, so the configuration returns with the data. Snapshot versus backup Flow in order: 1, Running VM to Guest agent freeze; 2, Guest agent freeze to CSI snapshot; 3, CSI snapshot to Backup on object storage; 4, CSI snapshot to Restore; 5, Backup on object storage to Restore. Service Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Running VM Service What it does: A guest with open files and memory state. If it fails or is weak: Copying the disk while it runs gives a torn copy. Guest agent freeze Step What it does: Pauses writes for a moment. If it fails or is weak: Without it the snapshot is crash consistent. CSI snapshot Data store What it does: A fast point in time copy on the same storage. If it fails or is weak: A storage failure removes disk and snapshot together. Backup on object storage Data store What it does: A copy in another place, made by Velero or an export. If it fails or is weak: An untested backup is only a hope. Restore Step What it does: Returns the VM from a snapshot or backup. If it fails or is weak: Restoring over a running VM can overwrite newer data. Backups that leave the cluster Velero with the KubeVirt plugin backs up VM objects and, with a CSI data mover, copies volume snapshot data to object storage. VirtualMachineExport serves a disk over HTTP for ad hoc copies. Schedule backups, set retention, and keep at least one copy in another failure domain. Test restores Schedule a restore into a test namespace each month and boot the VM. Check application data, not only that the VM starts. Incident: The backup on the same pool Incident A storage pool failed and both the disks and snapshots were lost. $ kubectl get volumesnapshotcontent -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,POOL:.status.snapshotHandle NAME POOL snapcontent-1 ceph-pool-a/csi-snap-77 $ ceph health HEALTH_ERR 1 pool inconsistent Every snapshot lived in the same Ceph pool as the disks it protected. The team added Velero backups to object storage in another site and a monthly restore test. Common mistakes Treating snapshots as backups Taking snapshots without the guest agent for databases Never testing a restore Restoring over a running VM without a plan"},"kubevirt/operating-kubevirt-monitoring-upgrades-and-troubleshooting":{"html":"\u003cp>A KubeVirt cluster has two layers to watch: the control plane that runs the VMs and the guests themselves. Knowing where to look first saves hours.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Metrics and alerts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrading the operator and the running VMs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A fixed troubleshooting order\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Day two checklist\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"monitoring\">Monitoring\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>KubeVirt exposes Prometheus metrics for its components and for each VMI, for example vCPU time, memory usage and network traffic. Alert on virt-api or virt-controller down, virt-handler not ready on a node, failed or stuck migrations, VMs stuck in Scheduling, and guests with sustained CPU throttling. Also watch the storage layer, since it is the usual source of slow VMs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"upgrades\">Upgrades\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Apply a newer operator manifest and the KubeVirt resource follows. The control plane components roll. Running VMs keep their old virt-launcher until restarted or migrated. Set \u003ccode>workloadUpdateStrategy.workloadUpdateMethods: [LiveMigrate]\u003c/code> so KubeVirt migrates VMs to launchers of the new version in batches. VMs that cannot migrate need a planned restart.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"op&gt;ctl;ctl&gt;old;ctl&gt;mig;old&gt;mig;mig&gt;new\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Upgrading KubeVirt without stopping VMs\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"op\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">New operator\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ctl\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">New control plane\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"old\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">VMs on old launchers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mig\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Batch live migration\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"new\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">VMs on new launchers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, New operator to New control plane; 2, New control plane to VMs on old launchers; 3, New control plane to Batch live migration; 4, VMs on old launchers to Batch live migration; 5, Batch live migration to VMs on new launchers.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"op\">\u003cdt>New operator \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Rolls the control plane to the new version.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipping versions can break the upgrade path.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ctl\">\u003cdt>New control plane \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> virt-api, virt-controller and virt-handler updated.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Check all pods are Ready before the next step.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"old\">\u003cdt>VMs on old launchers \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Still running the previous launcher version.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> They stay old until restarted or migrated.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mig\">\u003cdt>Batch live migration \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Moves VMs to new launchers a few at a time.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Non migratable VMs block and need a restart.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"new\">\u003cdt>VMs on new launchers \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The goal state with the new version everywhere.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Verify with the launcher image on each VMI.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"troubleshooting-order\">Troubleshooting order\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>kubectl get vm,vmi\u003c/code>: phase and conditions.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>kubectl describe vmi\u003c/code> and events: reasons for Pending or failed.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The virt-launcher pod: scheduling, image pull, OOMKilled.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The node and virt-handler logs.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Storage: PVC bound, DataVolume phase, CSI health.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Network: bindings, Multus, Service selectors.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The guest: \u003ccode>virtctl console\u003c/code>, guest logs, guest agent.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2 id=\"day-two-checklist\">Day two checklist\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Alerts in place, a tested drain, a tested restore, images versioned, upgrade rehearsed in a test cluster, and a record of which VMs cannot migrate.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-upgrade-that-left-old-launchers\">Incident: The upgrade that left old launchers\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Weeks after an upgrade, VMs still ran the old launcher image.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get vmi -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,LAUNCHER:.status.launcherContainerImageVersion\nkubectl get kv -n kubevirt kubevirt -o jsonpath='{.status.observedKubeVirtVersion}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get vmi -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,LAUNCHER:.status.launcherContainerImageVersion\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME    LAUNCHER\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web01   quay.io/kubevirt/virt-launcher:v1.2.0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get kv -n kubevirt kubevirt -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.status.observedKubeVirtVersion}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">v1.3.0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The control plane was on v1.3.0, but the VMs still used v1.2.0 launchers because no workload update method was set. Enabling LiveMigrate moved them over in a weekend window.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Assuming an operator upgrade upgrades the VMs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping alerts on stuck migrations\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Troubleshooting the guest before checking storage and scheduling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrading production first\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"monitoring","text":"Monitoring"},{"id":"upgrades","text":"Upgrades"},{"id":"troubleshooting-order","text":"Troubleshooting order"},{"id":"day-two-checklist","text":"Day two checklist"},{"id":"incident-the-upgrade-that-left-old-launchers","text":"Incident: The upgrade that left old launchers"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"KubeVirt monitoring","u":"https://kubevirt.io/user-guide/user_workloads/component_monitoring/"},{"t":"Updating KubeVirt","u":"https://kubevirt.io/user-guide/cluster_admin/updating_and_deletion/"},{"t":"Troubleshooting","u":"https://kubevirt.io/user-guide/user_workloads/debug_virtual_machines/"}],"text":"A KubeVirt cluster has two layers to watch: the control plane that runs the VMs and the guests themselves. Knowing where to look first saves hours. What you will learn Metrics and alerts Upgrading the operator and the running VMs A fixed troubleshooting order Day two checklist Monitoring KubeVirt exposes Prometheus metrics for its components and for each VMI, for example vCPU time, memory usage and network traffic. Alert on virt-api or virt-controller down, virt-handler not ready on a node, failed or stuck migrations, VMs stuck in Scheduling, and guests with sustained CPU throttling. Also watch the storage layer, since it is the usual source of slow VMs. Upgrades Apply a newer operator manifest and the KubeVirt resource follows. The control plane components roll. Running VMs keep their old virt-launcher until restarted or migrated. Set workloadUpdateStrategy.workloadUpdateMethods: [LiveMigrate] so KubeVirt migrates VMs to launchers of the new version in batches. VMs that cannot migrate need a planned restart. Upgrading KubeVirt without stopping VMs Flow in order: 1, New operator to New control plane; 2, New control plane to VMs on old launchers; 3, New control plane to Batch live migration; 4, VMs on old launchers to Batch live migration; 5, Batch live migration to VMs on new launchers. Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. New operator Step What it does: Rolls the control plane to the new version. If it fails or is weak: Skipping versions can break the upgrade path. New control plane Service What it does: virt-api, virt-controller and virt-handler updated. If it fails or is weak: Check all pods are Ready before the next step. VMs on old launchers Service What it does: Still running the previous launcher version. If it fails or is weak: They stay old until restarted or migrated. Batch live migration Step What it does: Moves VMs to new launchers a few at a time. If it fails or is weak: Non migratable VMs block and need a restart. VMs on new launchers Service What it does: The goal state with the new version everywhere. If it fails or is weak: Verify with the launcher image on each VMI. Troubleshooting order kubectl get vm,vmi : phase and conditions. kubectl describe vmi and events: reasons for Pending or failed. The virt-launcher pod: scheduling, image pull, OOMKilled. The node and virt-handler logs. Storage: PVC bound, DataVolume phase, CSI health. Network: bindings, Multus, Service selectors. The guest: virtctl console , guest logs, guest agent. Day two checklist Alerts in place, a tested drain, a tested restore, images versioned, upgrade rehearsed in a test cluster, and a record of which VMs cannot migrate. Incident: The upgrade that left old launchers Incident Weeks after an upgrade, VMs still ran the old launcher image. $ kubectl get vmi -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,LAUNCHER:.status.launcherContainerImageVersion NAME LAUNCHER web01 quay.io/kubevirt/virt-launcher:v1.2.0 $ kubectl get kv -n kubevirt kubevirt -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.status.observedKubeVirtVersion}&#x27; v1.3.0 The control plane was on v1.3.0, but the VMs still used v1.2.0 launchers because no workload update method was set. Enabling LiveMigrate moved them over in a weekend window. Common mistakes Assuming an operator upgrade upgrades the VMs Skipping alerts on stuck migrations Troubleshooting the guest before checking storage and scheduling Upgrading production first"},"kubevirt/migrating-from-vmware-to-kubevirt":{"html":"\u003cp>Many teams look at KubeVirt because of licensing changes on their current hypervisor. The technology move is manageable. The harder part is the people, process and tooling around the VMs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Inventory and migration waves\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tools that convert VMs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Drivers, networks and storage mapping\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What has no direct equivalent\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"inventory-and-waves\">Inventory and waves\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>List every VM with its operating system, size, disks, networks, dependencies, owner and downtime tolerance. Group them in waves: start with low risk Linux VMs, then application tiers, and leave the hardest (clustered databases, special hardware, old operating systems) for last. Each wave has a cutover plan, a test and a rollback.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"conversion\">Conversion\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>Migration Toolkit for Virtualization\u003c/strong> (upstream: Forklift) connects to vCenter, reads VMs and converts them into KubeVirt VirtualMachines. It uses \u003ccode>virt-v2v\u003c/code> to adapt the guest: it installs virtio drivers, removes VMware tools hooks and rewrites boot configuration. It supports a cold migration (VM off during the copy) and a warm migration (copy while running, then a short final sync).\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"inv&gt;plan;plan&gt;conv;conv&gt;test;test&gt;cut\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A migration plan, wave by wave\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"87.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"inv\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Inventory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"plan\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Migration plan\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"conv\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Conversion (Forklift)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"test\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Test in the cluster\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cut\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cutover and rollback\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Inventory to Migration plan; 2, Migration plan to Conversion (Forklift); 3, Conversion (Forklift) to Test in the cluster; 4, Test in the cluster to Cutover and rollback.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"inv\">\u003cdt>Inventory \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Every VM, its dependencies and its owner.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing a dependency breaks the application at cutover.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"plan\">\u003cdt>Migration plan \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Maps networks, storage and waves.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A wrong network map isolates the VM.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"conv\">\u003cdt>Conversion (Forklift) \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Copies disks and adapts the guest with virt-v2v.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A missing driver stops the guest booting.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"test\">\u003cdt>Test in the cluster \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Boots the VM, checks the application and performance.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipping the test moves the failure to cutover.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cut\">\u003cdt>Cutover and rollback \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Switches users to the new VM and keeps the old one for a while.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Deleting the source too early removes the rollback.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"drivers-networks-and-storage\">Drivers, networks and storage\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Linux guests usually have virtio drivers already. Windows guests need them installed, and boot problems usually mean a missing storage driver. Map each vSphere port group to a bridge or Multus network, and each datastore to a StorageClass. Replace VMware Tools with the qemu guest agent. Re-check IP addresses, MAC address based licences and monitoring agents.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"no-direct-equivalent\">No direct equivalent\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Distributed resource scheduling becomes the Kubernetes scheduler plus a descheduler. Backup moves from a VMware API product to CSI snapshots and Velero. Network virtualization such as NSX becomes network policies and CNI features. Plan training and runbooks, because operators will use kubectl instead of a vSphere client.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-windows-vms-that-would-not-boot\">Incident: The Windows VMs that would not boot\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Converted Windows VMs showed a blue screen after the first start.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virtctl vnc win01\nkubectl get vm win01 -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.domain.devices.disks[0].disk.bus}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virtctl vnc win01\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">STOP: 0x0000007B INACCESSIBLE_BOOT_DEVICE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get vm win01 -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.template.spec.domain.devices.disks[0].disk.bus}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">virtio\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The guests were converted to a virtio disk bus but no virtio storage driver was installed. The team installed the driver in the source VM before conversion, or temporarily used a SATA bus, then switched to virtio.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Migrating the largest VMs first\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deleting source VMs right after cutover\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting drivers for Windows guests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Planning only the VMs and not the operations around them\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"inventory-and-waves","text":"Inventory and waves"},{"id":"conversion","text":"Conversion"},{"id":"drivers-networks-and-storage","text":"Drivers, networks and storage"},{"id":"no-direct-equivalent","text":"No direct equivalent"},{"id":"incident-the-windows-vms-that-would-not-boot","text":"Incident: The Windows VMs that would not boot"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Forklift (Migration Toolkit for Virtualization)","u":"https://konveyor.github.io/forklift/"},{"t":"KubeVirt user guide","u":"https://kubevirt.io/user-guide/"},{"t":"virt-v2v manual","u":"https://libguestfs.org/virt-v2v.1.html"}],"text":"Many teams look at KubeVirt because of licensing changes on their current hypervisor. The technology move is manageable. The harder part is the people, process and tooling around the VMs. What you will learn Inventory and migration waves Tools that convert VMs Drivers, networks and storage mapping What has no direct equivalent Inventory and waves List every VM with its operating system, size, disks, networks, dependencies, owner and downtime tolerance. Group them in waves: start with low risk Linux VMs, then application tiers, and leave the hardest (clustered databases, special hardware, old operating systems) for last. Each wave has a cutover plan, a test and a rollback. Conversion The Migration Toolkit for Virtualization (upstream: Forklift) connects to vCenter, reads VMs and converts them into KubeVirt VirtualMachines. It uses virt-v2v to adapt the guest: it installs virtio drivers, removes VMware tools hooks and rewrites boot configuration. It supports a cold migration (VM off during the copy) and a warm migration (copy while running, then a short final sync). A migration plan, wave by wave Flow in order: 1, Inventory to Migration plan; 2, Migration plan to Conversion (Forklift); 3, Conversion (Forklift) to Test in the cluster; 4, Test in the cluster to Cutover and rollback. Data store Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Inventory Data store What it does: Every VM, its dependencies and its owner. If it fails or is weak: Missing a dependency breaks the application at cutover. Migration plan Step What it does: Maps networks, storage and waves. If it fails or is weak: A wrong network map isolates the VM. Conversion (Forklift) Step What it does: Copies disks and adapts the guest with virt-v2v. If it fails or is weak: A missing driver stops the guest booting. Test in the cluster Observability What it does: Boots the VM, checks the application and performance. If it fails or is weak: Skipping the test moves the failure to cutover. Cutover and rollback Step What it does: Switches users to the new VM and keeps the old one for a while. If it fails or is weak: Deleting the source too early removes the rollback. Drivers, networks and storage Linux guests usually have virtio drivers already. Windows guests need them installed, and boot problems usually mean a missing storage driver. Map each vSphere port group to a bridge or Multus network, and each datastore to a StorageClass. Replace VMware Tools with the qemu guest agent. Re-check IP addresses, MAC address based licences and monitoring agents. No direct equivalent Distributed resource scheduling becomes the Kubernetes scheduler plus a descheduler. Backup moves from a VMware API product to CSI snapshots and Velero. Network virtualization such as NSX becomes network policies and CNI features. Plan training and runbooks, because operators will use kubectl instead of a vSphere client. Incident: The Windows VMs that would not boot Incident Converted Windows VMs showed a blue screen after the first start. $ virtctl vnc win01 STOP: 0x0000007B INACCESSIBLE_BOOT_DEVICE $ kubectl get vm win01 -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.spec.template.spec.domain.devices.disks[0].disk.bus}&#x27; virtio The guests were converted to a virtio disk bus but no virtio storage driver was installed. The team installed the driver in the source VM before conversion, or temporarily used a SATA bus, then switched to virtio. Common mistakes Migrating the largest VMs first Deleting source VMs right after cutover Forgetting drivers for Windows guests Planning only the VMs and not the operations around them"}});